egin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e07baae1835d359b8826d64709546efbcadeebe234fecb72049908b235f14ab"} {"question" "q-97549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 & 5 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067114/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e0