 -4\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of $R^{3}$.\nThe matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: R^{2} → R^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x ∈ R^{2}$, are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$."] [17592186124333 :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{T}: V \\rightarrow W$ is a linear map and $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C} = \\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. Find the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the map $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to these bases, if\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)=-3\\mathbf{c}_1+5\\mathbf{c}_2,\\quad$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)=\\mathbf{c}_2-2\\mathbf{c}_1\\quad$ and $\\quad\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)=\\mathbf{c}_1+5\\mathbf{c}_2$."] [17592186124333 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the images of vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}T(b_{1}) =-3c_{1} + 5c_{2} & ⟹[T(b_{1})]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(b_{2}) = c_{2} - 2c_{1} & ⟹[T(b_{2})]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(b_{3}) = c_{1} + 5c_{2} & ⟹[T(b_{3})]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186124334 :question/problem "Consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ relative to these bases if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124334 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_i)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ for each $i=1,2,3$. We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & T(b_{1}) & T(b_{2})\\end{vmatrix}T(b_{3}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=(-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -1 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 4R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & -29 & 25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -29 & 25 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186124269 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the linear map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are shown above. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}}$."] [17592186124269 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{C} &= [T]_{B \\mid C} \\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124270 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ (shown above) and a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\nThe matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B} \\mid \\mathcal{C}}$ of the linear map $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to these bases, and the vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, are also given. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}}$."] [17592186124270 :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{C} &= [T]_{B \\mid C} \\cdot [x]_{B}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$. So, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 5; 4x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 2\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{C} &= [T]_{B \\mid C} \\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123281 17592186123282 17592186123283 17592186123284 17592186123285 17592186123287 17592186123288 17592186123289 17592186123290 17592186123291 17592186124037 17592186124038 17592186124039 17592186124040 17592186124041] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123280 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123280 :answer-field/id] [17592186123280 :answer-field/key] [17592186123280 :answer-field/type] [17592186123286 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123286 :answer-field/id] [17592186123286 :answer-field/key] [17592186123286 :answer-field/type] [17592186124036 :answer-field/correct] [17592186124036 :answer-field/id] [17592186124036 :answer-field/key] [17592186124036 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
